      "writtenOn": "2022-04-29T19:16:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I suppose it could, but it\u0027s bigger (needs three pointers) and supports resize, which I was trying to avoid. The fact that C++ doesn\u0027t have a vocabulary type for a runtime owning fixed size container with an associated size is silly.\n\nOriginally this was important while I was developing this because I had Vars inside SkSVGColor directly. In the common case there aren\u0027t any vars and I wanted to keep the overhead on SkSVGColor minimal. Unfortunately, one must store at least a pointer and a length (or resort to linked lists which should be avoided). So to keep the overhead to a minimum of one pointer I created Vars. But now the extra overhead of another pointer is fairly minimal, so one could use std::vector here. It\u0027s just so inelegant. Also SkSVGColor needs to be copyable so I would still want the std::vector in a reference counted thing so it doesn\u0027t keep getting copied. But that means dragging in std::shared_ptr it\u0027s pain, which would also be nice to avoid.\n\nEven with this setup I end up default constructing a bunch of SkString (fortunately fairly fast) just so I can initialize this from last to first without moving everything around. Maybe I\u0027ll think about this for a minute.",
      "parentUuid": "44ea6227_a1067257",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 215,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 225,
        "endChar": 22
      },
